 nursing & midwiferyx2538-399Xw(DLC)   2025228039w(OCoLC)135122862740uhttps://journals.sbmu.ac.ir/en-jnm/index  aAUTH02816nam a2200433 i 9918986606206676DNLM20251113110711.0240407s2024    inu      b    001 0 eng    a  2024010070  a1409620788a1464878227  a9781612499628qhardcover  a1612499627qhardcover  a9781612499635qpaperback  a1612499635qpaperback  z9781612499642qelectronic book  z9781612499659qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1409605885z(OCoLC)1409620788z(OCoLC)1464878227  aLBSORbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdYDXdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  9United States00aRA1199.4.A46bP37 202400aW 20.55.A500a615.9/07223/eng/202404191 aParascandola, John,d1941-eauthor.12aA history of the development of alternatives to animals in research and testing /cJohn Parascandola. 1aWest Lafayette, Indiana :bPurdue University Press,c[2024]  axvi, 176 pages ;c23 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aNew directions in the human-animal bond  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aAlternatives before the three Rs -- Russell, Burch, and the three Rs -- An underwhelming response : the 1960s -- Increased attention to alternatives : the 1970s -- Alternatives come of age : the 1980s.  a"Growing public interest in animal welfare issues in recent decades has prompted increased attention to the efforts to develop alternative, nonanimal methods for use in biomedical research and product testing. In A History of the Development of Alternatives to Animals in Research and Testing, the first book-length study of the subject, John Parascandola traces the history of the concept of alternatives to the use of animals in research and testing in Britain and the United States from its beginnings until it had become firmly established in the scientific and animal protection communities by the end of the 1980s. This account of the history of alternatives is set within the context of developments within science, animal welfare, and politics. The book covers the key role played by animal welfare advocates in promoting alternatives, the initial resistance to alternatives on the part of many in the scientific community, the opportunity provided by alternatives for compromise and cooperation between these two groups, and the dominance of the "Three Rs"-reduction, refinement, and replacement"--cProvided by publisher.12aAnimal Use Alternativesxhistory22aAnimals, Laboratory 0aNew directions in the human-animal bond  aAUTH03319nam a2200493 i 9918986562906676DNLM20251114111421.0230607t20242024nyua   f b    001 0 eng    a  2024401204  aGBC3G30212bnb  a1381203786  a9781032102627q(hbk)  a9781032081038q(pbk)  z9781003214472q(ebk)  z9781003801771qePub ebook  z9781003800378qPDF ebook  a1032102624q(hbk)  a1032081031q(pbk)  z1003214479q(ebk)  a(OCoLC)1381181414z(OCoLC)1381203786  aYDXbengerdacYDXdBDXdOCLCOdUKMGBdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdUUMdDLCdIG#  apcc  9United States 4aRA773.6b.A374 202400aWA 59004a610.6962231 aAgarwal, Vinita,eauthor.10aHealth communication for social justice :ba whole person activist approach /cVinita Agarwal. 1aNew York, NY ;aAbingdon, Oxon :bRoutledge, an imprint of the Taylor & Francis Group, an informa business,c2024. 4c©2024  axviii, 402 pages :billustrations (black and white) ;c26 cm  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier1 aRoutledge social justice communication activism series  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.00tForeword /rGary L. Kreps --tPreface --gSection ItConceptualizing health:tA whole person framework of health ;tModels for representing health and disease ;tA social justice activist approach to health --gSection IItConstructing health:tEvolving understandings of health ;tHealthcare relationships ;tHealthcare systems ;tHealth literacy --gSection IIItContextualizing health:tHealth and the environment ;tTechnology and health ;tHealth, religion, and spirituality ;tGlobal health.  a"This textbook combines whole person and social justice perspectives to educate students on the role of communication in promoting inclusive and person-centered healthcare practices. This book explores health inequities experienced by disadvantaged and marginalized populations and outlines the actions students can take to address these challenges. The book demonstrated how physical, mental, and emotional health is connected to equitable understandings of individual, community, and environmental health. It considers how social, interpersonal, and systemic factors such as personal relationships, language, literacy, religion, technology, and the environment affect health equity. To present strategies and invite action to support the goals of the whole person, social justice activist approach, the book provides contemporary examples, interviews with communication scholars, and case studies that examine local communities and the everyday contexts of health meaning making. This textbook serves as a core or supplemental text for graduate and upper-level undergraduate courses in health communication."--Back cover of paperback edition.12aHealth Communication22aSocial Justice22aPhysician-Patient Relations 0aRoutledge social justice communication activism series  aAUTH01489cas a2200481 a 9604509DNLM20251121115804.0cr|cn#||||||||961107c19919999caufr p       0    0eng    asn 94023183 0 a1090-3941l1090-3941  a(DNLM)SR0080759(s)  a(OCoLC)29761128  aMiUcMiUdDNLM  9United States00aW1bSU76910aSurg Technol Int2dnlm 0aSurgical technology international00aSurgical technology international.  a[London] :bCentury Press,cc1991-3 aSan Francisco, CA :bUniversal Medical Press  av. :bcol. ill.  aTwo no. a year,b2000-  aBiennial,b1991-1993  aAnnual,b<1994>-1999?  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1