, PA :bTaylor & Francis  av. :bill. ;c25 cm.  aBimonthly  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 1, no. 1 (Jan./Feb. 1989)-  aTitle from cover.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 17, no. 1 (Jan./Feb. 2005).  aAlso issued online.12aOils, Volatile22aPlant Oils 2aPeriodical0 x2163-81524 uhttp://www.tandfonline.com/toc/tjeo20/current  aAUTH02736nam a2200421 i 9918986574706676DNLM20251124111535.0230426s2024    enk      b    001 0 eng    a  2023011086  a9781032158471q(hardback)  a1032158476q(hardback)  a9781032158457q(paperback)  a103215845Xq(paperback)  z9781003245919q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1380466215  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCLdYDXdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  9England00aRC451.4.P79bA33 202400aWM 42000a616.89/14223/eng/202305261 aAdams, Mariec(Psychotherapist),eauthor.14aThe myth of the untroubled therapist :bprivate life, professional practice /cMarie Adams.  aSecond edition. 1aMilton Park, Abingdon, Oxon ;aNew York, NY :bRoutledge,c2024.  a175 pages ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aPrevious edition: 2014.  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"Therapists are not immune to the range of problems their clients experience, including divorce, bereavement, illness, and depression. The Myth of the Untroubled Therapist considers what kinds of difficulties clinicians face and considers the best ways of dealing with them. Featuring interviews from forty different practitioners - CBT, psychoanalytic, integrative and humanistic therapists from an international array of backgrounds - on how they coped during times of personal strife, the book dispels the myth that therapists are immune to the kind of problems that they help clients through. Using clinical examples, personal experience and research literature, Marie Adams challenges mental health professionals to take a step back and consider their own well-being. This new edition is updated throughout and includes a new chapter looking at the impact of Covid-19 on practitioners. Linking therapists' personal histories to their choice of career, the book highlights some of the key elements that may serve, and sometimes undermine, counsellors working in private practice or mental health settings. The Myth of the Untroubled Therapist is ideal for counsellors and psychotherapists as well as social workers and those working within any kind of helping profession"--cProvided by publisher.12aPsychotherapy12aHealth Personnelxpsychology 2aCase Reports  ihas work:aThe myth of the untroubled therapist (Text)4https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/ontology/hasWork  aAUTH01396nam a2200337 i 101776550DNLM20251113114626.0210226r18841884maua          000 0 eng    aDNLMbengerdacDNLMdDNLM  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5510 no.51 aParmele, George L.,eauthor.10aConcerning records /cby Geo. L. Parmele. 1a[Springfield, Mass.?] :b[publisher not identified],c[1884?]  a13 pages :billustrations ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Presented at the Second Annual Meeting of the New England Dental Society, Providence, R.I., October 4, 1883."--Page 1.  aReprint from: New England journal of dentistry, Springfield, Mass., 1884.  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aNLM copy, author's printed presentation slip tipped in before page 1.5DNLM  aWill digitizec202504072pda5DNLM  aNeeds condition reviewc202504075DNLM12aForms and Records Control22aDental Records22aLibrary Science0 aNew England journal of dentistry.  aAUTH00947nam a2200301 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390400025000800420008001050440012001130600033001251000040001582450069001982640044002673000027003113360026003383370028003643380027003925000017004195000077004365040041005136000043005546500015005976510010006126550014006229990009006369918751767806676DNLM20251118101920.0240129r18841884gw       b    000 0bger    aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Germany00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5629 no.151 aTollin, Henri,d1833-1902,eauthor.10aAndreas Caesalpin /cvon Lic. theol. Dr. med. hon. Henri Tollin. 1aBonn :bVerlag von Emil Strauss,c1884.  apages 295-390 ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aCover title.  a"Separat-Abdruck aus Pflüger's Archiv f.d. ges. Physiologie, Bd. XXXV."  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aCesalpino, Andrea,d1524 or 1525-1603.12aPhysicians 2aItaly 2aBiography  aAUTH03850nam a2200445 i 9918986571506676DNLM20251114095703.0231121s2024    nyu      b    001 0 eng    a  2023023422  a1400973118  a9781479825363qhardcover  a1479825360qhardcover  a9781479825370qpaperback  a1479825379qpaperback  z9781479825400qelectronic book  z9781479825424qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1400971737z(OCoLC)1400973118  aDLCbengerdacDLCdYDXdBDXdOCLCOdMNNdYDXdOCLCOdPSCdIG#  apcc  9United States00aRA390.A2bB47 202400aWA 530.100a362.1223/eng/202401251 aBerry, Nicole S.,d1970-eauthor.10aGood intentions in global health :bmedical missions, emotion, and health care across borders /cNicole S. Berry. 1aNew York :bNew York University Press,c[2024]  axviii, 181 pages ;c23 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aAnthropologies of American medicine: culture, power, and practice  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aIntroduction: Intimate connections and the making of global health -- Transformations in global health : short-term medical missions and their appeal to North American clinicians -- Global health as profoundly personal : race, kinship, and volunteering -- Transformative moments: "Body Knowledge" and ethical action -- Lack of context : why we rely on visceral ethics in DIY global health work -- Where there is no doctor : ethical lives in the absence of feedback -- Conclusion: Affect and critical global health research that matters.ethi  a"In the past two decades, medical missions have gained popularity among medical professionals, who view these excursions as important ethical interventions. Indeed, the notion of giving back by volunteering in rural or impoverished communities is celebrated as an ideal act of selflessness, one whose effects are unquestionably beneficial to those being served.Good Intentions in Global Health is a groundbreaking exploration of the growing realm of informal global health engagement, shedding light on the intricate interplay between intentions, emotions, and ethical considerations. Drawing on fieldwork in Guatemala, Nicole S. Berry investigates those who volunteer for short-term medical missions, revealing how the intent to do good shapes their everyday understandings of their own actions taken in the global health domain.Berry uncovers how the glorification of medical missions can obscure problems that stem from North American clinicians doctoring in places where they typically do not understand the context. The short-term nature of missions also means that volunteers are not privy to the long-term effects of their actions―the potential harms that may arise from a lack of sustained follow-up care or the utter absence of documentation that they were even there. By relying on gut instincts to reassure themselves that they are doing good, volunteers often bypass a comprehensive assessment of the ethical dimensions underlying their global health work.Good Intentions in Global Health shows why desires and emotions are increasingly important to contemporary global health. She makes the case that we must pay attention to volunteers’ perceptions of their work, however wrongheaded or naïve, in order to truly influence global health on the ground"--cProvided by publisher.12aGlobal Health22aMedical Missionsxethics22aHealth Services Accessibility 0aAnthropologies of American medicine  aAUTH01230nam a2200337 i 9918226580806676DNLM20251124151647.0100922r18831882mau           000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)664943744  aUKRCSbengcUKRCSdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCAerda  an-us-ma  9United States00aW6bP3 v.7481 box 5577 no.161 aAdams, J. F. Alleyneq(James Forster Alleyne),d1844-1914eauthor.14aThe New Lenox malaria case /cby J.F.A. Adams. 1aCambridge :bPrinted at the Riverside Press,c1883.  a16 pages ;c19 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Reprinted from the Boston Medical and Surgical Journal of December 28, 1882"--Wrapper.  aWill not digitizec20221024lscanned by another institution2pda5DNLM22aSmith Paper Co. (Lee, Mass.).12aMalariaxetiology22aDisease Outbreaks22aDrainage, Sanitaryxlegislation & jurisprudence22aWater Pollution 2aMassachusetts0 aBoston medical and surgical journal.  aAUTH04492nam a2200409 i 9918991791406676DNLM20251120084202.0230928t20232023sz ac    b    001 0 eng    a9783031195631q(hbk.)  a3031195639q(hbk.)  z9783031195648qebook  a(OCoLC)1400208395  aAMUbengerdacAMUdOCLCOdAMUdBDXdOCLCQ  apcc  9Switzerland 4aQR46b.M53 202300aQW 10004a616.9/041qOCoLC223/eng/2023020900aMicrobiome in 3P medicine strategies :bthe first exploitation guide /cNadiya Boyko, Olga Golubnitschaja, editors. 1aCham :bSpringer,c[2023]. 4c©2023  axv, 425 pages :billustrations (some color), portraits ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aAdvances in predictive, preventive and personalised medicine ;vvolume 16  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.0 aChapter 1: Microbiome in the framework of Predictive, Preventive and Personalised Medicine -- Chapter 2: Artificial Intelligence-based Predictive, Preventive, and Personalised Medicine applied to Bacteraemia Diagnosis -- Chapter 3: Vaginal microbiome and its role in HPV induced cervical carcinogenesis -- Chapter 4: Microbiome in lean Individuals: Phenotype-specific Risks and Outcomes -- Chapter 5: Microbiome and Obesity -- Chapter 6: Pathophysiology-based approach for individualized use of probiotics and prebiotics for metabolic syndrome: implementing predictive, preventive, and personalized medical approach -- Chapter 7: Selection of prebiotic substances for individual prescription -- Chapter 8: Probiotic Administration for the Prevention and Treatment of Gastrointestinal, Metabolic and Neurological Disorders -- Chapter 9: Microbial therapy with indigenous bacteria. From idea to clinical evidence -- Chapter 10: Fecal microbiota transplantation in diseases not associated with Clostridium difficile: current status and future therapeutic option -- Chapter 11: Personalized microbiome correction by application of individual nutrition for type 2 diabetes treatment -- Chapter 12: Pro-, pre- and synbiotic supplementation and oxalate homeostasis in 3PM context: focus on microbiota oxalate-degrading activity -- Chapter 13: In vitro study of specific properties of probiotic strains for effective and personalized probiotic therapy -- Chapter 14: Probiotic concepts of predictive, preventive, and personalized medical approach for obesity: Lactic acid bacteria and Bifidobacteria probiotic strains improve glycemic and inflammation profiles -- Chapter 15: Oral Microbiome and Innate Immunity in Health and Disease: Building a Predictive, Preventive and Personalized Therapeutic approach.  aThe book analyses microbiome-relevant findings focused on clinical needs providing the roadmap to implement recent achievements in the area representing a valuable contribution to the paradigm shift from reactive to predictive, preventive and personalised medicine (PPPM / 3PM) considered as the most advanced concept in medicine. Already well-acknowledged as well as future advantages of application of pre-, pro- and pharma-biotics are detailed in the book. Socio-economic impacts of the area are considered in the context of the entire spectrum of healthcare services from disease care provided to patients up to health care provided to persons in suboptimal health conditions. Innovative technologies including phenotyping, genotyping, individualised profiling, patient stratification, big data analysis, and multi-omics, amongst others are all involved in the book. The book is unique from view point of multi-professional expertise involved. International network presents more than 10 countries worldwide including Belgium, China, Germany, Israel, Ukraine and USA. The data presented are of great scientific value and of particular importance for educating a broad spectrum of professionals including researchers, healthcare givers, policy makers, business people, policy makers and general population.12aMicrobiota12aPrecision Medicine12aPreventive Medicine1 aBoyko, Nadiya,eeditor.1 aGolubnitschaja, Olga,eeditor. 0aAdvances in predictive, preventive and personalised medicine ;vv.16.  aAUTH01243nam a2200361 i 9918991784506676DNLM20251113151350.0221001s2023    sz a     b    000 0 eng    aGBC2M81072bnb  a9783031200243qhardbound  a3031200241qhardbound  z9783031200250qebook  a(OCoLC)1346293253  aYDXbengerdacYDXdBDXdUKMGBdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCOdOHX  apcc  9Switzerland00aWB 35004a615.72231 aMacheras, P.q(Panos),eauthor.10aRevising oral pharmacokinetics, bioavailability and bioequivalence based on the finite absorption time concept /cPanos Macheras, Athanasios A. Tsekouras. 1aCham :bSpringer,c2023.  axv, 129 pagesbillustrations (black and white, and colour) ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aAdministration, Oral12aBiological