nces  a(OCoLC)39656086  aDNLMcDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9United States00aWM 270bVC no.143 199800aChanging lives /ca presentation of Films for the Humanities & Sciences ; a production of Public Affairs Television, Inc. ; a presentation of Thirteen/WNET New York.  aPrinceton, N.J. :bFilms for the Humanities & Sciences,cc1998.  a1 videocassette (81 min.) :bsd., col. ;c1/2 in.  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  avideocassettebvf2rdacarrier1 aClose to home  aExecutive editors, Bill Moyers, Judith Davidson Moyers.8 a(Producer)  While addiction may cause similar changes in the brains of different people, recovery is a very individual solution.  Changing behavior is the aim of treatment, but no single treatment program will work for all addicts.  This program visits the Ridgeview Institute near Atlanta to interview recovering addicts and sit in on a group therapy session.  The program also visits Project Safe, an innovative treatment program that reaches out to disadvantaged mothers who are addicts, and to their children who are at serious risk of becoming addicts.12aSubstance-Related Disordersxtherapy22aPsychotherapy, Group 2aPersonal Narrative1 aMoyers, Bill,d1934-2025.2 aFilms for the Humanities (Firm) 0aClose to home (Series)  aHISRELb19991201  aAUTH04016nam a2200421 i 9918917720606676DNLM20251114104653.0240509t20242024fr ac    b    000 0cfre    a9782336452104q(paperback)  a2336452103q(paperback)  a(OCoLC)1433985637  aAUXAMbengerdacAUXAMdLGGdOCLCOdLGGdOCLCOdPUL  apcc  9France 4aRK41b.R53 202400aWZ 112.5.D314a6171 aRiaud, Xavier,eauthor.10aCowboys, Indiens et--dentistes :bune autre façon de voir le Far West /cXavier Riaud. 1aParis :bL'Harmattan,c2024. 4c©2024  a228 pages :billustrations, portraits ;c22 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aMédecine à travers les siècles,x1953-910X  aIncludes bibliographical references (page 211-213).00tBalbutiements. --t1840 : An I des institutions dentaires américaines --tGardner Quincey Colton (1814-1898), Horace Wells (1815-1848) et l'anesthésie au protoxyde d'azote --tDr Samuel S. White (1822-1879) --tGuerre de Sécession (1861-1865) --tLa dentisterie dans la Confédération --tLa dentisterie dans l'Union --tLe scorbut au sein des armées --tMémoires d'un dentiste confédéré --tLes difficultés de la manufacture dentaire Brown & Hape --tSimples soldats --tDr Josephus Requa (1833-1910) --tDr Edward Maynard (1813-1891) --tDr Henry Parr (1843-1932) --tDr William T. Green Morton (1819-1868) --tDr James Baxter Bean (1834-1870) --tLa chirurgie maxillo-faciale de guerre --tJohn Wilkes Booth (1838-1865) et Lewis Thomton Powell (1844-1865) : identifications controversées de deux conjurés sudistes responsables de la mort d'Abraham Lincoln --tLes dents d'Abraham Lincoln (1809-1865) --tLe cancer d'Ulysses S. Grant (1822-1885) --tBrièvement, la dentisterie d'après-guerre... --tHommage aux premiers dentistes noirs américains --tWestern --tDoc Holliday (1851-1887) : un dentiste au Far West --tJesse James --tLe dentiste équin de Buffalo Bill --tCalamity Jane --tGénéral Custer, un maniaque --tGrat Dalton --tLes frères Younger --tBilly le Kid --tWild Bill Hickok --tWyatt Earp --tSquirre Tooth Alice --tIron Teeth --tWilliam Quantrill --tHygiène dentaire au Far West --tLa reddition du chef apache Cochise --tIdentification des corps à Little Big Horn --tDr Will Frackelton --tWilliam Saunders, le premier dentiste de West Point --tLes dents des Indiens.  a"Les westerns hollywoodiens des années 1960-70 nous montrent des héros, hommes ou femmes, aux sourires blancs, aux dents bien rangées... mais la réalité semble avoir été tout autre. Xavier Riaud revient brièvement sur les institutions dentaires créées aux États-Unis avant la guerre de Sécession (1861-1865), leur fonctionnement pendant et les praticiens qui ont eu un impact direct sur le conflit. Il s'interroge aussi sur l'état bucco-dentaire des plus grandes figures du Far West comme Billy le Kid, Jesse James, Calamity Jane, etc. et sur celui bien sûr des Indiens. Parsemé d'anecdotes (le dentiste équin de Buffalo Bill, les Indiens eux-mêmes, Doc Holliday, dentiste et joueur de poker, grand ami de Wyatt Earp, etc.), ce livre revient notamment sur l'identification bucco-dentaire des soldats décédés à Little Bighorn (1876), la plus grande victoire indienne sur les tuniques bleues. Nostalgie ou passion pour l'histoire américaine, cet opus a été écrit avec le souvenir des couleurs présentes dans les westerns de John Wayne, Gary Cooper, Errol Flynn..."--Page 4 of cover.12aDentists22aHistory of Dentistry22aFamous Persons22aHistory, 19th Century 2aUnited States 2aBiography 0aMédecine à travers les sièclesx1953-910X  aAUTH01192cam a2200289 i 450000100100000000300050001000500170001500800410003203500200007304000350009304400180012806000230014610000580016924501580022726400650038530000440045033600260049433700280052033800270054854001420057558300390071758300720075665000160082865000250084465000240086999900090089304330060RDNLM20251120142035.0810622s1912    nyua          000 0 eng    a(OCoLC)14805142  aDNLMbengerdad20251120dDNLM  9United States00aQC 173bC913f 19121 aCrehore, Albert C.q(Albert Cushing),d1868-eauthor.14aThe family-tree arrangement of the elements on the corpuscular ring theory together with an analysis of the cause of gravitation /cby Albert C. Crehore. 1aYonkers, New York :b[Albert C. Crehore Laboratory?],c1912.  aiii, 76 pages :billustrations ;c25 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aThe National Library of Medicine believes this item to be in the public domain.uhttps://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/5DNLM  aWill digitizec202011062pda5DNLM  aCondition reviewedc20251120lconservation not necessary2pda5DNLM12aGravitation22aElementary Particles22aModels, Theoretical  aAUTH04598nam a2200541 i 9918986613106676DNLM20251121081921.0220208s2021    caua     b    000 0 eng    a  2021427323  a1291310191a1291363280a1291368164  a9781977408198qpaperback  a1977408192qpaperback  a(OCoLC)1291392574z(OCoLC)1291310191z(OCoLC)1291363280z(OCoLC)1291368164  aYDXbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdOCLCLdOCLCO  alccopycatapcc  an-us---  9United States00aRJ505.C63bJ393 202100aWM 172.504a618.92/8914252231 aJaycox, Lisa,eauthor.10aCognitive Behavioral Intervention for Trauma in Schools (CBITS) for American Indian youth /cNational Native Children's Trauma Center, Lisa H. Jaycox, Audra K. Langley, Sharon A. Hoover.3 aCBITS for American Indian youth 1aSanta Monica, Calif. :bRAND Corporation,c[2021]  axxvi, 140 pages :billustrations ;c28 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  a"Developed by the National Native Children's Trauma Center as an adaption of Cognitive behavioral intervention for trauma in schools - 2nd edition".  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 138-140).0 aGROUP SESSION 1: Introductions -- GROUP SESSION 2: Education and Relaxation -- INDIVIDUAL SESSION 1: Trauma Narrative: Processing the Trauma Memory -- INDIVIDUAL SESSION 2: Trauma Narrative: Processing the Trauma Memory -- INDIVIDUAL SESSION 3: Trauma Narrative: Processing the Trauma Memory -- GROUP SESSION 3: Introduction to Cognitive Therapy -- GROUP SESSION 4: Combating Unhelpful Negative Thoughts -- GROUP SESSION 5: Introduction to Real-Life Exposure -- GROUP SESSION 6: Exposure to Stress or Trauma Memory -- GROUP SESSION 7: Exposure to Stress or Trauma Memory -- GROUP SESSION 8: Introduction to Problem-Solving -- GROUP SESSION 9: Practice with Problem-Solving -- GROUP SESSION 10: Relapse Prevention and Graduation -- SESSION 1: Caregiver Education Program -- SESSION 2: Caregiver Education Program -- SESSION 1: Teacher Education Program.  aExposure to traumatic events among youth is relatively common. Almost all youth experience initial distress as a reaction to such events, but, for most, natural resilience causes the distress to gradually subside. However, a substantial minority continue to experience distress in the months after trauma exposure. The Cognitive Behavioral Intervention for Trauma in Schools (CBITS) program was designed for use with groups of students who have experienced significant traumatic experiences and are suffering from related emotional or behavioral problems, particularly symptoms of posttraumatic stress disorder. Delivered by school-based clinicians and taking into account cultural context, it uses a variety of proven cognitive behavioral techniques in an early intervention approach, including psychoeducation about trauma and its consequences, relaxation training, learning to monitor stress or anxiety levels, recognizing maladaptive thinking, challenging unhelpful thoughts, social problem-solving, creating a trauma narrative and processing the traumatic event, and facing trauma-related anxieties rather than avoiding them. CBITS focuses primarily on three goals: decreasing current symptoms related to trauma exposure, building skills for handling stress and anxiety, and building peer and caregiver support. In this new adaptation, a team of experts convened by the University of Montana adapted the CBITS program for American Indian youth, weaving in culturally appropriate and meaningful concepts about resilience and healing while maintaining CBITS' core cognitive-behavioral skill-building techniques.--cSource other than the Library of Congress.  aAlso available on the internet via WWW in PDF format.  aSee also RAND/TL-272.12aStress Disorders, Post-Traumaticxtherapy12aCognitive Behavioral Therapyxmethods22aViolencexpsychology22aSchool Mental Health Services22aAmerican Indian or Alaska Native22aCulturally Competent Care22aChild22aAdolescent 2aUnited States1 aLangley, Audra,eauthor.1 aHoover, Sharon A.,eauthor.2 aNational Native Children's Trauma Center,eauthor.  aAUTH00984nam a2200301 i 45000010017000000030005000170050017000220080041000390200018000800400025000980420008001230440012001310600010001432450080001532460021002332500023002542640064002773000040003413360026003813370028004073380027004356500022004626500025004846510012005097000039005217100113005609990009006739918935829506676DNLM20251119064145.0241106s2020    uy a     b    000 0 spa    a9789974312777  aDNLMbengerdacDNLM  apcc  9Uruguay00aW 70000aMedicina legal :bderecho médico /ccoordinador, Hugo Rodríguez Almada.1 aDerecho médico  aSegunda edición. 1aMontevideo :bOficina del Libro FEFMUR,cdiciembre de 2020.  a308 pages :billustrations ;c21 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier12aForensic Medicine22aLegislation, Medical 2aUruguay1 aRodríguez Almada, Hugo,eeditor.2 aUniversidad de la República (Uruguay).bDepartamento de Medicina Legal y Ciencias Forenses,eissuing body.  aAUTH01372nam a2200409 i 9918863680606676DNLM20251119065441.0231111t20232023gw c     b    001 0 ger    a9783662652275q(pbk.)  z9783662652282q(eBook)3 a9783662652275  a(OCoLC)1409135674  aOHXbengerdacOHXdOCLCQ  apcc  9Germany00aWT 145 7aRC2lcco 7aHQ2lcco04a618.97/89qOCoLC223/eng/2024041700aPsychotherapeuten und das Altern :bdie Bedeutung des Alterns in der therapeutischen Beziehung und der eigenen Lebensgeschichte /cBernhard Strauss, Carsten Spitzer, Hrsg. 1aBerlin, Germany :bSpringer,c[2023] 4c©2023  axxv, 374 pages :bportrait ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aPsychotherapie: Praxis,x2570-3285  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aAgingxpsychology12aPsychotherapeutic Processes22aPsychotherapistsxpsychology22aAgedxpsychology1 aStrauss, Bernhard,d1956-eeditor.1 aSpitzer, Carsten,eeditor. 0aPsychotherapie: Praxisx2570-3293  aAUTH06062nam a2200397 i 9918991791106676DNLM20251120085103.0240413t20242024sz a     b    000 0 eng    a9783031605444qhardcover  a3031605446qhardcover  z9783031605451qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1429657797  aYDXbengerdacYDXdYDXdOCLCOdHF9  apcc  9Switzerland04aRA394b.P74 202400aWA 3004a362.1068223/eng/2024062400aPreserving health, preserving Earth :bthe path to sustainable healthcare /cPranav Kumar Prabhakar, Walter Leal Filho, editors. 1aCham, Switzerland :bSpringer, an imprint of Springer Nature Switzerland AG,c[2024] 4c©2024  aviii, 303 pages :billustrations (chiefly color). ;c24 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aWorld sustainability series,x2199-7373  aIncludes bibliographical references.0 aPreface / Pranav Kumar Prabhakar and Walter Leal Filho -- Chapter 1 : Confronting the Health Impact of Climate Change : A Comprehensive Exploration /  Aniruddha Sen and Ayan Chatterjee -- Chapter 2  : Sustainable Practices in The Pharmaceutical Industry : Development and Adoption / Koyel Kar, Sailee Chowdhury, Priyanka Chakraborty, and Arpa Saha -- Chapter 3 : Climate Change and Health / Prabhakar Singh, Mohammad Murtaza Mehdi, Saqub Hassan, Kunal Biswas, and V. Ramesh Kumar -- Chapter 4 : Healing with Care : A Roadmap to Sustainable Healthcare / Keshav Singh, Vivek Kumar Srivastava, and Ashish Shukla -- Chapter 5 : Community Engagement and Education for Eco-conscious Health / Abhinay Thakur and Ashish Kumar -- Chapter 6 : Human Health in the Face of Extreme Weather : An Analysis of Impacts and Implications /Geeta Arora, Vandna Chhabra and K. Akshit -- Chapter 7 : Exploring the Emergence of Sustainable Practices in Healthcare Research and Application as a Path to a Healthier Future / Harshita Jain -- Chapter 8 : Sustaining Sustainable: Investigating the Full Spectrum of Food Waste, From Production Through Consumption to Disposal / Leena N. Fukey and Kedarnath Rajaram -- Chapter 9 : Navigating Health Challenges: Approaches for Sustainable Development in a Changing Climate / Amir Ahmad Dar, Mehak Malhotra and Akshat Jian -- Chapter 10 : Mental Health and Well-being in Sustainable Practices / Vivek Barik, M.D. Samsuddin, Piyali Khamkat, and Bhakti Bhusan Barik -- Chapter 11 : Unani Medicine's Integration in Healthcare : Harmonizing Sustainable Food Wisdom for Holistic Well-being / M. S. F. Sapra -- Chapter 12 : Advances in Cosmetic Products Towards A New Future / Parnika Neytal, Nilima Maji, and Suman Maji -- Chapter 13 : The role of analytics in improving health outcomes : Are Accredited social health activists (ASHA) at the forefront in social health protection? / Vijayetta Sharma -- Chapter 14 : Mindful Approaches to Sustainable Healthcare : Nurturing Mental Health and Well-Being / Rahul Saxena, Suyash Savena and Ajit Pal Singh -- Chapter 15 : From Ideation to Innovation : Integrating Pharmaceutical Innovation and Sustainable Development / Heba M. Mansour and Aiman S. El-Khatib -- Chapter 16 : Artificial Intelligence for advanced Sustainable development goals : A 360-degree approach / Rahul Joshi, Krishna Pandey, and Suman Kumari  aThis book is a groundbreaking book that delves into the critical intersection of health care and sustainability. As the healthcare industry faces increasing scrutiny regarding its environmental impact, this comprehensive guide offers a roadmap for transforming healthcare systems into sustainable entities that prioritize the health of both individuals and the planet.   Drawing on a multidisciplinary approach, the book explores the environmental footprint of healthcare practices and infrastructure, shedding light on the resource consumption, waste generation, and pollution associated with the industry. It establishes the crucial link between environmental health and human health, emphasizing how a polluted environment can impact public health outcomes and the need to preserve natural resources for improved healthcare outcomes.  The book goes beyond merely highlighting the challenges ; it presents practical strategies and actionable solutions for healthcare professionals, policymakers, and stakeholders to navigate the path toward sustainability. It examines case studies and best practices from healthcare institutions that have successfully implemented sustainable initiatives, showcasing their approaches and lessons learned.   In addition to addressing the technical aspects, the book tackles the ethical and social considerations of sustainable health care. It emphasizes the importance of equity, social justice, and community engagement in promoting sustainability within healthcare delivery and decision-making processes.   This book is an indispensable resource for healthcare professionals, policymakers, researchers, and students seeking to integrate sustainability practices into their work. It serves as a guide for creating a greener and healthier future, highlighting the potential of sustainable health care to improve both environmental well-being and public health outcomes.   With its comprehensive coverage, insightful analysis, and practical recommendations, this book contributes to the global conversation on sustainable health care and provides a solid foundation for transforming the industry. It inspires readers to become catalysts for change, fostering a new era of health care that not only preserves health but also preserves the Earth for future generations.  -- Provided by publisher.12aHealth Care Sector12aSustainable Development1 aKumar Prabhakar, Pranav,eeditor.1 aLeal Filho, Walter,eeditor. 0aWorld sustainability seriesx2199-7373.  aAUTH01338nam a2200409 i 9918986609206676DNLM20251125092941.0230511t20242024enka     b    001 0deng    aGBC3929932bnb  a9781800131903qpaperback  a1800131909qpaperbac