83235.0240501s2024    ne a     b    001 0 eng    a  2024020392  a9789004700864qhardcover  a9004700862qhardcover  z9789004700871qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1430654348  aICU/DLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCOdCUYdOCLCOdYDXdOCLCOdIG#  apcc  af-ua---  9Netherlands00aRA418.3.E3bA87 202400aWZ 70 HE300a362.10932223/eng/202406261 aAustin, Anne Eliese,eauthor.10aHealthmaking in ancient Egypt :bthe social determinants of health at Deir el-Medina /cby Anne E. Austin.30aSocial determinants of health at Deir el-Medina 1aLeiden ;aBoston :bBrill,c[2024]  axii, 263 pages :billustrations (some color) ;c24 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aCulture and history of the ancient Near East,x1566-2055 ;vvolume 138  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages [222]-256) and index.0 aIntroduction -- A new theological approach to ancient health -- The place, the community, and the evidence -- Finding health and stress at Deir el-Medina -- Forging social support -- Accessing healthcare -- Maintaining economic stability -- Conclusion -- Appendix : the osteological datasets.  a"This book explores the health of ancient Egyptians living in the New Kingdom village of Deir el-Medina. Through an interdisciplinary approach that combines skeletal analysis with textual evidence, the book examines how social factors, such as social support, healthcare access, and economic stability, played crucial roles in buffering individuals from stress and promoting good health. This is the first, comprehensive book on the bioarchaeology of Deir el-Medina including data from human remains spanning the site's New Kingdom occupation. This book highlights how the Social Determinants of Health can be used to explain how past people maintained their health"--cProvided by publisher.12aSocial Determinants of Healthxhistory12aHistory, Ancient22aHistory of Medicine 2aEgypt, Ancient 0aCulture and history of the a