TH01395cas a2200409 i 9918989696106676DNLM20251124083220.0m     o  d        cr#|||||||||||250207c20259999sz || poo     0    0eng    a  2025227957  a3059-3247l3059-3247  a(OCoLC)1513109145  aDNLMbengerdad20251124cDNLMdDNLM  apcc  9Switzerland00aW110aDiscov Dev Biol2dnlm00aDiscover developmental biology. 1a[Cham, Switzerland] :bSpringer Nature,c[2025]-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aBegan with Volume 235, issue 1 (June 2025).0 aPubMedbSelected citations only  aArticles are published online as received.  aAlso issued in print.0 aVolume 235, issue 1 (June 2025); title from journal homepage (SpringerLink, viewed March 26, 2025).1 aVolume 235, issue 1 (June 2025) (viewed March 26, 2025).12aDevelopmental Biology 2aPeriodical0 x3059-323900tDevelopment genes and evolutionx0949-944Xw(DLC)sn 96031146w(OCoLC)3518269540uhttps://link.springer.com/journal/427  aAUTH04233pam a2200397 i 9918986640706676DNLM20251125112059.0230308s2024    njua     b    001 0 eng    a  2023004401  a9781119756392q(hardback)  a1119756391q(hardback)  z9781119756408q(epdf)  z9781119756415q(epub)  z9781119756422q(ebook)  a(OCoLC)1380463408  aDLCbengerdacDLCdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCLdYDXdIG#  apcc  9United States00aRC280.D5bG3783 202300aWI 14900a616.99/43223/eng/202305260 aGastrointestinal oncology (Jankowski)10aGastrointestinal oncology :ba critical multidisciplinary team approach /cedited by Janusz A. Z. Jankowski, University College London, London, UK  aSecond edition. 1aHoboken, NJ :bWiley Blackwell,c2024.  axvi, 615 pages :billustrations ;c29 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"The paradigm of a journey from observation to hypothesis testing and then onward to fulfil Koch's postulates. The discovery 40 years ago of the Helicobacter Pylori organism was a paradigm shift in many ways. Perhaps most importantly as it indicated that the host and the organism have a complex interaction which can lead to disease, not only chronic infection, autoimmunity, but also several forms of cancer. As an adjunctive benefit it showed that we should question any disease causation assumptions which are not evidence based, Stress as a cause of ulcers being the best example. The journey was long in that it took time to assemble the epidemiological, bacteriological, clinical, oncological teams. The result was, and still is, to provide the only cure for a condition in gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease. The benefits also included alleviation of dyspepsia, cures of low grade gastric MALTOMA's and prevention of epithelial tumors of the gastric antrum. This discovery has spawned new interest in fields of research outside the infection inflammation cancer sequence, including probiotics, antibiotic resistance, and modulation of host-bacterial interactions. As I reflect on my career's narrative above, I am therefore glad to see such a large array of multidisciplinary experts working together in this endeavour of Gastrointestinal Oncology: A Critical Multidisciplinary Team Approach. Cancers of the gastrointestinal tract (GIT) encompassing esophagus, stomach and colorectum make up a significant proportion of cancers diagnosed worldwide and contribute to a considerable burden of disability-adjusted life years and years of life lost. (Kocarnik et al., 2022) The increase of GIT malignancies has been linearly associated with the human development index (HDI), likely due to the adoption of an affluent lifestyle with an increase in the consumption of processed meat, fast food, sedentary lifestyle, smoking, and increasing alcohol consumption. Obesity has become a worldwide pandemic and has been linked to the development of GIT cancers through multiple genetic and epigenetic microenvironment changes. Excess adipokines in obesity mediate a state of chronic inflammation resulting in the activation of multiple pathways that promote carcinogenesis. The understanding of the epidemiology and predisposing factors to GIT cancers is crucial in the development of early detection strategies. Screening efforts such as colonoscopy has reduced the burden of CRC worldwide. Similarly, screening of gastric cancers which has been adopted in Japan and Korea has reduced the numbers as well as improved the survival of gastric cancer. In this chapter, we will discuss the epidemiology, global time trends, burden of disease, risk factors of esophageal, gastric and intestinal cancer and strategies to reduce the incidence of these malignancies"--cProvided by publisher.12aGastrointestinal Neoplasms22aDigestive System Neoplasms1 aJankowski, Janusz A. Z.,eeditor.  aAUTH01493cam a2200457 i 9918986604606676DNLM20251125073229.0t|230716s2024    enk      b    001 0 eng    aGBC4195082bnb  a1390554278  a9781032361185qhbk  a1032361182qhbk  a9781032361161qpbk  a1032361166qpbk  z9781003330332qebook  z9781003823032qePub ebook  z9781003823018qPDF ebook8 aCIPO000003877  a(OCoLC)1390555764z(OCoLC)1390554278  aYDXbengerdad20251125dBDXdOCLCOdOCLCQdUKMGBdOCLCOdDNLM  apcc  9England00aWM 460.204a150.19/5092qOCoLC223/eng/202311301 aStefana, Alberto,eauthor.10aMarion Milner :ba contemporary introduction /cAlberto Stefana and Alessio Gamba. 1aAbingdon, Oxon :bRoutledge,c2024.  avi, 122 pages ;c20 cm.  atext2rdacontent  aunmediated2rdamedia  avolume2rdacarrier1 aRoutledge introductions to contemporary psychoanalysis  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.12aMilner, Marion,d1900-1998.12aPsychoanalytic Theory22aPsychoanalysisxhistory1 aGamba, Alessio,eauthor. 0aRoutledge introductions to contemporary psychoanalysis.  aAUTH02147nam a2200481 i 9918986624306676DNLM20251124113348.0240114s2024    