 a  2008185157  a1660-4431l0300-5186  a(OCoLC)60693882  aMMUcMMUdNLMdDNLM  anlmcopyc  9Switzerland00aW1bMO568C10aFront Neurol Neurosci2dnlm00aFrontiers of neurology and neuroscience.  aBasel ;aNew York :bKarger,c2005-  av. :bill. ;c25 cm.  aAnnual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier0 aVol. 19-1 aMEDLINEbv20, 2005-v45, 20211 aPubMedbv20, 2005-v45, 2021 ; Selected citations only before this date  aAlso issued online.12aNervous System Diseases22aNeurosciences0 x1662-280400tMonographs in clinical neurosciencex1420-2441w(OCoLC)379684664 uhttp://www.karger.com/BookSeries/Home/230638  aAUTH02041nam a2200373 i 9918991789106676DNLM20251113083424.0230120s2023    sz ab    b    001 0 eng    aGBC3210052bnb  a9783031264696qhardback  a303126469Xqhardcover  z9783031264702qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)1368268762  aUKMGBbengerdacUKMGBdBDXdOCLCQdOCLCFdYDXdOCLCOdOHX  apcc  af-tz---  9Switzerland04aSB317.B23bR37 202300aSB 317.B2304a583/.77509678223/eng/202308081 aRashford, John,eauthor.10aBaobab :bthe Hadza of Tanzania and the baobab as humanity's tree of life /cJohn Rashford. 1aCham, Switzerland :bSpringer Nature Switzerland,c[2023]  axxix, 382 pages :bcolor illustrations, color maps ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index.  a"Modern humans, descendants of a founding population that separated from chimpanzees some five to eight million years ago, are today the only living representative of a branching group of African apes called hominins. Because of its extraordinary size and shape, the baobab (Adansonia digitata L.) has long been identified as the most striking tree of Africas mosaic savanna, the landscape generally regarded as the environment of hominin evolution. This book makes the case for identifying the baobab as the tree of life in the hunter-gatherer adaptation that was the economic foundation of hominin evolution. The argument is based on the significance of the baobab as a resource-rich environment for the Hadza of northeastern Tanzania, who continue to be successful hunter-gatherers of the African savanna." --cProvided by publisher.12aAdansonia12aEast African People 2aTanzania  aAUTH01299nam a2200409 i 9918991789606676DNLM20251113142647.0240727t20232023si a     b    000 0 eng    a9789819995844qhardbound  a9819995841qhardbound  z9789819995851qebook  a(OCoLC)1450202443  aOHXbengerdacOHX  apcc  9Singapore00aW 82 7aRA2lcco 7aQ2lcco1 aGuo, Shuli,d1973-eauthor.10aAdvanced technologies in healthcare :bAI, signal processing, digital twins and 5G /cShuli Guo, Lina Han, Yanan Guo. 1aSingapore ; [Berlin] :bSpringer,c[2023] 4c©2023  axvi, 170 pages :billustrations (chiefly color) ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references.12aBiomedical Technology22aArtificial Intelligence22aSignal Processing, Computer-Assisted22aDiagnostic Imaging22aCloud Computing22aComputer Simulation1 aHan, Lina,d1973-eauthor.1 aGuo, Yanan,d2000-eauthor.  aAUTH01309cas a2200397 a 101584167DNLM20251120134535.0cr|cn#||||||||120514d20122015ja || p       |    0eng    a  2012212012  a2186-8026l2186-8026  a(OCoLC)806221228  aDLCbengcDLCdDLCdOCLCOdDLCdDNLM  apcc  9Japan00aW1bRA162AJ00aRadiation emergency medicine.1 aREM  aAomori, Japan :bHirosaki University Press  aSemiannual  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aBegan with vol. 1, no.1/2 (Mar. 2012);ceased with vol. 4, no. 2 (Aug. 2015).  aAlso issued online.  aDescription based on: Vol. 1, no.1/2 (Mar. 2012); title from title page.  aLatest issue consulted: Vol. 1, no.1/2 (Mar. 2012).12aRadiation Injuries22aEmergenci